KN5000 control-panel serial — OPTION-A VERIFICATION INSTRUMENTATION
==================================================================

What this is
------------
The probe set that made the option-C / option-B / option-A adjudications possible, plus the
two runtime knobs that isolated option A's root cause.  It is *instrumentation only*: it
changes no emulated behaviour except where a knob is explicitly set in the environment.

It lived only in an ephemeral session scratchpad until 2026-07-21; it is preserved here
because **A' cannot be verified to this record's standard without it**.

459 lines, 3 files:
  src/mame/matsushita/kn5000_cpanel.cpp            (+185)
  src/devices/cpu/tlcs900/tmp94c241_serial.cpp     (+233)
  src/devices/cpu/tlcs900/tmp94c241_serial.h       (+41)

Read `notes/kn5000-cpserial-measurement-discipline.md` before trusting any number this
prints.  Two of the labels below are wrong; see "TWO OF ITS OWN LABELS ARE WRONG".

How to apply
------------
This is a patch **on top of option A**, not on top of HEAD:

    git apply notes/kn5000-cpserial-sender-handshake-candidate.patch
    git apply notes/kn5000-cpserial-sender-handshake-instrumentation.patch

Verified on 2026-07-21: HEAD + the candidate patch reproduces the option-A source tree
byte for byte, and this patch then applies clean on top of it.  For A' (= A minus the
rule-5 deferral in `idle_detect_callback`, and minus/guarded the `abandon_inta_cycle()`
rewind), apply both and then remove the block — or just set `KN5A_NOIDLEWAIT=1`, which is
exactly what that knob does.

The runtime knobs (this is the main reason to keep the patch)
-------------------------------------------------------------
* `KN5A_NOIDLEWAIT=1` — disables ONLY the late "defer the INTA request while the receiver
                   is still armed" rule (option A's rule 5).  This is the knob that
                   isolated A's regression to a single block, in two independent repros:
                   with it set, `x_sim2` and `pfx3` return to the pristine screens.
* `KN5A_PRE=1`   — disables the four panel-side option-A sites and prints
                   `MODE=PRE (option A DISABLED)`.  **It is NOT a validated pre-change
                   control.**  It reproduced the pristine binary exactly on `a1`
                   (`d9f02718` x6) and `b3` (`f2b583c5` x6), but on `b1` the pristine
                   binary is DEAD (`f2b583c5` x6) while `KN5A_PRE=1` is LIVE and
                   byte-identical to full option A.  The serial device's `rx_ready`
                   fan-out is not gated by it, and this race turns on sub-bit-time phase.
                   Use it to localise a mechanism; use the published binary
                   (md5 52818738929b4056179d115d8ca1ad7f) as the control.
* `KN5A_TRACE=1` — per-event cpanel tracing (verbose; for mechanism, not for gating).

What it prints
--------------
One `KN5A| ...` summary block per run, at `device_stop()` on serial channel 1, plus a
periodic `KN5A| HB t=...` heartbeat line.  Summary shape:

    KN5A| ===== control-panel serial link summary (option A) =====
    KN5A| drop_ext        = 0     <-- panel-clocked bits LOST; must be 0 under A
    KN5A| drop_int        = 7664   (/8 = 958)
    KN5A| tx_bytes        = 958    (CPU command bytes)
    KN5A| cpanel_recv     = 958    (bytes the panel assembled)
    KN5A| PHANTOM ARITHMETIC drop_int/8 == tx_bytes == cpanel_recv : HOLDS
    KN5A| strand_entry    = 0      (gate closed MID-BYTE)
    KN5A| strand_exit_mid = 0      (MIDBYTE-REOPEN)
    KN5A| PHASE_HIST      = 7:152
    KN5A| cp_qmax         = 21   cp_busreq = ...   cp_abandon = ...

★ TWO OF ITS OWN LABELS ARE WRONG — FIX THEM BEFORE REUSING THE PATCH
---------------------------------------------------------------------
The summary text was written before the adjudication and encodes two claims the
adjudication falsified.  Keep the counters; do not keep the labels.

1. `drop_ext ... must be 0 under A` — **`drop_ext` is a TAUTOLOGY under option A.**  The
   panel emits a clock edge only when `IOC && RXE`, tested in the same callback, while the
   RX latch gate is the weaker `IOC` alone; `receive_armed()` therefore implies
   `ext_clock` by construction and the counter cannot increment.  It read a clean 0 in
   runs where the panel was DEAD and dispatching button presses nobody made.  Delete it
   from any successor's acceptance gate.

2. `bprime_FIRED ... must be 0 (else A incomplete)` — **B'-inertness is BLIND to the
   sender-side failure mode.**  Measured: `bprime_calls = 2096`, `bprime_FIRED = 0` in an
   `x_sim2` run containing 28 mid-byte strands and 28 MIDBYTE-REOPENs.  A zero from it is
   not evidence of anything.  (Proposing it as a sharp self-check for A was the main
   agent's error; it is recorded here so nobody trusts it again.)

The counters that ARE trustworthy — they can be embarrassed
-----------------------------------------------------------
* `strand_entry` / `strand_exit_mid` / `strand_exit_cln` — the receive gate sampled at the
  *register write*, independent of edge sampling, so they see the hazard even with the fix
  disabled.  `wclose = wopen + 1` in a wedged run is the signature of the mid-byte close
  being the last gate event of the session.
* `PHASE_HIST` — the *sender's* own bit position (`kn5000_cpanel::m_tx_clock_count`)
  sampled at the instant the CPU begins assembling a byte.  Intact framing = ONE bucket.
  This is the non-tautological framing oracle; nothing else in the set is.
* `tmr_dead` — free-running baud-generator edges (kn5000-30's livelock signature).
* `cp_qmax` — deepest the panel's TX queue got (A's design argued it could not exceed 21;
  `x_multi` reached 466).
* `max_rearm` — longest single wait for the bus grant, in 4 us ticks.
* `cp_hold` — falling edges where the panel held its shift register because the receiver
  was shut: 7,424-10,224 real bits per run that the shipped build silently destroys.
* `drop_int / tx_bytes / cpanel_recv` — kn5000-30's phantom arithmetic.  Valid **only on
  live runs**: dead edges inflate `drop_int`.  The `phantom` counter itself is a tautology
  (`sioclk()` only latches when the gate is open, so "a byte latched with the gate shut"
  is impossible to count); this arithmetic is the real evidence.
* `RX_LAST` / `CPTX_LAST` (in the option-B instrumentation, not this one) — last 64 bytes
  received vs sent; direct proof of rotation.

And above all of them: **the pixel diff of the snapshot PNGs.**  See
`notes/kn5000-cpserial-measurement-discipline.md` and `notes/kn5000-cpserial-repros/README.md`.

Is the instrumentation itself outcome-neutral?
----------------------------------------------
Where it was checked, yes: `x_multi` and `x_sim2` produce the SAME six snapshot md5s on the
instrumented build and on the clean option-A build (`484418d3` x6 and `7322ac60` x6
respectively), and `x_sim2` repeated on the clean build is byte-identical.  Neutral on
three schedules is not neutral in general — see the `KN5A_PRE` caveat above.

Not included here
-----------------
The option-B and option-C instrumented sources (also scratchpad-only) and the A+B'
variant, which differed from this one in `tmp94c241_serial.cpp` alone: it added B''s actual
behaviour (`if (!ext_clock) { m_rx_clock_count = 8; m_rx_shift_register = 0; }`) next to
the `bprime_*` counters this patch already carries.  B' is a documented one-liner and its
diagnostic is blind, so it was not worth a second preserved patch.

